## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a DP over partitions using prefix sums and a hashmap to achieve linear-time partition counting. This also exercises both bottom-up and top-down reasoning, and edge-case handling for K and negative numbers.

## Key Concepts

- Prefix sums
- Hash map DP
- Edge-case handling (K=0, negative numbers)

## Code Example

```javascript
function countPartitions(A, K) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const N = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= N; i++) prefix[i] = prefix[i-1] + A[i-1];
  const dp = new Array(N+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  const map = new Map();
  map.set(0, 1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= N; i++) {
    const key = prefix[i] - K;
    const ways = map.get(key) ?? 0;
    dp[i] = ways;
    const cur = (map.get(prefix[i]) ?? 0) + dp[i];
    map.set(prefix[i], cur % MOD);
  }
  return dp[N] % MOD;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt to count partitions with an additional constraint, e.g., every block length <= L?
- What if A contains large numbers and K is dynamic per query?

## Why This Is Asked
Intersects BFS path counting and graph pruning under constraints; tests meticulous edge-case handling.

## Key Concepts
- BFS for unweighted shortest paths
- Path counting with modulo arithmetic
- Handling blocked nodes and unreachable cases
- Complexity: O(N+M)

## Code Example
```javascript
function shortestPaths(n, edges, blocked, s, t) {
  const g = Array.from({ length: n }, () => []);
  for (const [v, u] of edges) if (!blocked[v] && !blocked[u]) g[v].push(u);
  const dist = Array(n).fill(Infinity);
  const ways = Array(n).fill(0);
  if (blocked[s] || blocked[t]) return { dist: -1, ways: 0 };
  dist[s] = 0; ways[s] = 1;
  const q = [s];
  while (q.length) {
    const v = q.shift();
    for (const u of g[v]) {
      if (dist[u] === Infinity) {
        dist[u] = dist[v] + 1; ways[u] = ways[v]; q.push(u);
      } else if (dist[u] === dist[v] + 1) {
        ways[u] = (ways[u] + ways[v]) % 1000000007;
      }
    }
  }
  return { dist: dist[t] === Infinity ? -1 : dist[t], ways: ways[t] };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to weighted graphs and use Dijkstra with path counting?
- How to adapt for multiple sources and answering many queries efficiently?

